                <pb facs="0237" n="137" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg137"/>

                <head>No. 89.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morton to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-01-23"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 23, 1884.</date>
                        (Received February 9.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 486.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to your telegram received
                    January 2, requesting me to ask that the time allowed for the admission of
                    American pork in France be extended to the 1st of February, I have the honor to
                    inform you that the application was made and granted.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>LEVI P. MORTON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
